look at ~/Document/Project Archive/chezz for it's /ideate command, gives an interactive session the intention of developing creative concepts while deferring implementation to asynchronous batches. this is something that realisateur itself should own, across projects, not scheduler. realisateur should be responsible for wiring projects up with an /ideate capacity, learning principles and applying them across projects. this also opens the question of whether scheduler's -i interaction is proper. it seems scheduler should be more about adding next action items to a list. -i can remain and hook in realisateur to decide whether the idea is actionable or instead a job for realisateur to help incubate a vision. that means realisateur both incubates visions that are fresh, and also helps foster big ideas spawned from other contexts
